1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,990
Monday, August ninth, nineteen
sixty-five. Noon.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:12,010
In a radio and television studio, a forty-one-year-old
prime minister is answering reporters' questions.

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:13,550
And then he stops.

4
00:00:14,246 --> 00:00:18,116
"For me, it is a moment of
anguish," Lee Kuan Yew says.

5
00:00:18,726 --> 00:00:22,706
"Would you mind if we stop for a while."
The recording is switched off,

6
00:00:22,746 --> 00:00:24,756
so he can pull himself together.

7
00:00:25,364 --> 00:00:28,614
That morning, his island had been
pushed out of Malaysia.

8
00:00:29,224 --> 00:00:31,074
It was now a country of its own.

9
00:00:31,444 --> 00:00:32,854
Almost no army.

10
00:00:33,224 --> 00:00:34,634
No natural resources.

11
00:00:35,044 --> 00:00:36,414
And barely any land.

12
00:00:36,897 --> 00:00:41,467
Minutes earlier, he had raised the problem
that would shape everything after.

13
00:00:42,237 --> 00:00:47,197
"Our water supply comes from Johore." The
country's water came from the neighbor

14
00:00:47,237 --> 00:00:48,787
that had just let it go.

15
00:00:49,281 --> 00:00:52,851
Today, that island is one of the
richest countries on Earth.

16
00:00:53,221 --> 00:00:55,131
And it's not even the same shape.

17
00:00:55,521 --> 00:00:58,931
Since that day, Singapore has grown
by more than a quarter.

18
00:00:59,381 --> 00:01:01,911
It built its own land, out of the sea.

19
00:01:02,436 --> 00:01:04,746
So how does a country make itself bigger?

20
00:01:05,316 --> 00:01:07,346
Where does all that sand come from?

21
00:01:07,696 --> 00:01:09,826
Why did its neighbors stop selling it?

22
00:01:10,096 --> 00:01:14,326
And what is Singapore building now, to stop
the sea from taking it all back?

23
00:01:14,839 --> 00:01:17,869
The obvious answer is: just build up.

24
00:01:18,279 --> 00:01:19,449
But here's a surprise.

25
00:01:20,079 --> 00:01:23,229
New York City is actually more
crowded than Singapore.

26
00:01:23,879 --> 00:01:26,249
Singapore just has nowhere else to go.

27
00:01:26,499 --> 00:01:27,969
So it did something stranger.

28
00:01:31,996 --> 00:01:33,166
Start with the map.

29
00:01:33,776 --> 00:01:39,326
In nineteen sixty-five, Singapore covered about five
hundred and eighty square kilometers.

30
00:01:39,956 --> 00:01:42,946
That's about two hundred and
twenty-five square miles,

31
00:01:43,316 --> 00:01:45,846
about three quarters the size
of New York City.

32
00:01:46,351 --> 00:01:50,481
Inside that space, it had to fit
everything a country needs.

33
00:01:51,411 --> 00:01:56,301
Homes, factories, a port, an
airport, an army, and water.

34
00:01:56,816 --> 00:01:57,886
And it was packed.

35
00:01:58,616 --> 00:02:02,506
Many people lived in crowded wooden
villages called kampongs.

36
00:02:03,176 --> 00:02:08,836
In May nineteen sixty-one, a fire at Bukit Ho
Swee burned more than two thousand of

37
00:02:08,876 --> 00:02:14,586
those houses in a single afternoon, and around
sixteen thousand people lost their homes.

38
00:02:15,193 --> 00:02:19,683
The new Housing and Development Board
rehoused them in new concrete flats,

39
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:22,263
and it became the model for
the whole country.

40
00:02:23,013 --> 00:02:27,983
Today, about three in four Singapore residents
live in a government-built flat.

41
00:02:28,506 --> 00:02:30,796
But those flats come with a twist.

42
00:02:31,406 --> 00:02:34,176
You own your home on a
ninety-nine-year lease.

43
00:02:34,626 --> 00:02:37,916
When the lease runs out, the flat
goes back to the state.

44
00:02:38,466 --> 00:02:40,596
Even your home is borrowed land.

45
00:02:41,104 --> 00:02:44,234
With six million people today and
nowhere to spread,

46
00:02:44,864 --> 00:02:47,094
Singapore had only one direction left.

47
00:02:47,584 --> 00:02:49,194
Outward, into the sea.

48
00:02:51,712 --> 00:02:54,642
At first, Singapore made land
out of itself.

49
00:02:55,172 --> 00:03:00,692
From nineteen sixty-six, engineers cut down
the hills of Bedok and Tampines and

50
00:03:00,732 --> 00:03:04,062
dumped the earth into the shallow
sea along the East Coast.

51
00:03:04,662 --> 00:03:09,582
That one project took about thirty years and
added more than fifteen square

52
00:03:09,622 --> 00:03:13,272
kilometers, about six square
miles, of new coast.

53
00:03:13,882 --> 00:03:19,282
Today there are highways, apartment towers and a
long beach park on top of what used

54
00:03:19,322 --> 00:03:20,112
to be water.

55
00:03:20,639 --> 00:03:21,969
Then came the airport.

56
00:03:22,559 --> 00:03:27,979
In April nineteen seventy-five, Lee Kuan Yew
approved a plan to build a brand-new

57
00:03:28,019 --> 00:03:31,349
airport at Changi, on the far
east of the island.

58
00:03:31,859 --> 00:03:34,049
Most of it would have to be built on sea.

59
00:03:34,546 --> 00:03:40,506
Five giant dredgers dug up the seabed offshore
and pumped forty-one million cubic

60
00:03:40,546 --> 00:03:46,586
meters of sand and mud, enough to fill the Empire
State Building almost forty times,

61
00:03:46,626 --> 00:03:48,656
through ten kilometers of pipe.

62
00:03:49,213 --> 00:03:53,383
Pek Hock Thiam, on the
reclamation team, later said:

63
00:03:53,913 --> 00:03:58,063
who would believe we could create so much
land in two and a half years?

64
00:03:58,643 --> 00:04:02,573
Changi Airport opened on July
first, nineteen eighty-one.

65
00:04:03,383 --> 00:04:06,733
The first flight landed at ten past
seven in the morning.

66
00:04:07,383 --> 00:04:10,493
Today it's one of the most famous
airports in the world,

67
00:04:10,803 --> 00:04:12,913
and it's still growing into the sea.

68
00:04:13,465 --> 00:04:18,645
Downtown, the hotel with the boat on top and
the giant gardens with their steel

69
00:04:18,685 --> 00:04:23,155
trees stand on land reclaimed from
the nineteen seventies on.

70
00:04:23,665 --> 00:04:25,555
So does much of the Marina Bay skyline.

71
00:04:26,254 --> 00:04:28,884
And then, Singapore ran out of hills.

72
00:04:29,494 --> 00:04:31,584
To keep growing, it needed sand.

73
00:04:31,974 --> 00:04:33,104
A lot of sand.

74
00:04:33,254 --> 00:04:34,344
From somewhere else.

75
00:04:36,868 --> 00:04:41,188
Sand sounds like the one thing the
world will never run out of.

76
00:04:41,228 --> 00:04:45,038
But the right kind of sand is
surprisingly hard to find.

77
00:04:45,548 --> 00:04:49,358
For building and reclamation, you
need sand from rivers,

78
00:04:49,708 --> 00:04:51,198
beaches and the seabed.

79
00:04:51,704 --> 00:04:54,414
And Singapore bought more of
it than anyone.

80
00:04:54,964 --> 00:04:59,614
A United Nations report in twenty nineteen
found that over twenty years,

81
00:04:59,824 --> 00:05:03,474
it imported five hundred and
seventeen million tonnes.

82
00:05:03,864 --> 00:05:06,994
By far the largest sand
importer on the planet.

83
00:05:07,568 --> 00:05:08,938
It came from its neighbors.

84
00:05:09,648 --> 00:05:12,918
Malaysia, Indonesia, Cambodia, Vietnam.

85
00:05:13,608 --> 00:05:19,308
In Indonesia, officials said up to twenty-six
small islands had been lost to erosion

86
00:05:19,348 --> 00:05:23,258
by two thousand nine, and many
blamed the sand trade.

87
00:05:23,778 --> 00:05:26,538
And the numbers didn't add up. A U.N.

88
00:05:26,578 --> 00:05:31,398
study found that Singapore reported importing
about a hundred and twenty million

89
00:05:31,438 --> 00:05:36,128
tonnes more Indonesian sand than
Indonesia said it had exported.

90
00:05:36,736 --> 00:05:39,346
One by one, the neighbors said stop.

91
00:05:40,116 --> 00:05:44,246
Malaysia banned sand exports to
Singapore in nineteen ninety-seven.

92
00:05:45,216 --> 00:05:48,036
Indonesia banned sea sand in
two thousand three,

93
00:05:48,076 --> 00:05:53,036
and tightened it in two thousand seven, when it
was supplying about ninety percent

94
00:05:53,076 --> 00:05:54,326
of Singapore's sand.

95
00:05:54,877 --> 00:05:56,287
Prices shot up.

96
00:05:56,517 --> 00:05:59,267
So Singapore opened its
national stockpile:

97
00:05:59,817 --> 00:06:02,487
hills of sand kept in reserve
for emergencies.

98
00:06:03,037 --> 00:06:06,347
Where they are, and how big they
are, isn't published.

99
00:06:06,909 --> 00:06:10,479
Vietnam and Cambodia followed
with bans of their own.

100
00:06:10,829 --> 00:06:15,239
And in twenty nineteen, Malaysia
stopped all sea sand exports.

101
00:06:15,809 --> 00:06:20,339
By then it had been supplying more than
ninety-seven percent of Singapore's imports.

102
00:06:20,984 --> 00:06:23,034
The sand is starting to flow again.

103
00:06:23,624 --> 00:06:26,294
Indonesia lifted its ban in
twenty twenty-three.

104
00:06:26,644 --> 00:06:29,134
But Singapore had already
learned its lesson.

105
00:06:29,544 --> 00:06:32,454
It needed ways to make land
with less sand.

106
00:06:35,012 --> 00:06:37,922
The first trick was to join
islands together.

107
00:06:38,512 --> 00:06:41,862
Off the southwest coast there were
seven small islands.

108
00:06:42,392 --> 00:06:44,462
Singapore filled in the sea between them.

109
00:06:44,923 --> 00:06:48,473
Together, they had covered less than
ten square kilometers.

110
00:06:49,223 --> 00:06:55,573
Today, Jurong Island is about thirty, and it's packed
with oil refineries and chemical plants.

111
00:06:56,147 --> 00:06:58,237
The second trick was to go down.

112
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:03,327
Under the seabed next to Jurong Island,
there are giant caverns,

113
00:07:03,367 --> 00:07:07,757
a hundred and thirty meters deep, about
four hundred and thirty feet,

114
00:07:08,087 --> 00:07:11,997
holding one point four seven
million cubic meters of oil.

115
00:07:12,387 --> 00:07:14,417
That's more than nine million barrels.

116
00:07:15,049 --> 00:07:19,459
Putting that oil underground freed up about
sixty hectares on the surface,

117
00:07:19,709 --> 00:07:21,599
around a hundred and fifty acres.

118
00:07:22,209 --> 00:07:27,049
At the opening in twenty fourteen, the prime
minister said Singapore's land problem

119
00:07:27,089 --> 00:07:29,239
was a little bit like peak oil.

120
00:07:29,772 --> 00:07:33,482
And sometimes, making land
meant moving people.

121
00:07:34,292 --> 00:07:37,982
The island of Pulau Tekong once had
more than ten villages.

122
00:07:38,492 --> 00:07:41,922
In the nineteen eighties, everyone
was moved to the mainland.

123
00:07:42,472 --> 00:07:48,342
In nineteen eighty-six, navy landing craft even
carried a temple's gods across the water.

124
00:07:48,832 --> 00:07:51,262
And the third trick came from
the Netherlands.

125
00:07:51,312 --> 00:07:55,212
On Pulau Tekong, Singapore built a polder.

126
00:07:55,763 --> 00:07:58,953
Instead of filling the sea all
the way up with sand,

127
00:07:59,523 --> 00:08:02,473
engineers built a ten-kilometer
wall in the sea,

128
00:08:02,783 --> 00:08:05,853
about six miles long and up to
six meters high,

129
00:08:06,103 --> 00:08:08,433
and pumped the water out from behind it.

130
00:08:08,863 --> 00:08:11,813
The new land sits about one
point two meters,

131
00:08:12,123 --> 00:08:14,313
around four feet, below sea level.

132
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:17,131
It took close to half as much sand.

133
00:08:17,561 --> 00:08:20,411
But it needs pumps to stay dry, forever.

134
00:08:21,221 --> 00:08:23,531
The Dutch project director put it simply:

135
00:08:24,021 --> 00:08:28,271
the essence of a polder is that you need
pumps to take the water out,

136
00:08:28,421 --> 00:08:29,971
so the land stays dry.

137
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:34,410
The Tekong polder was announced complete in
September twenty twenty-five.

138
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:39,810
It added about eight hundred hectares, and
it's used by the army for training.

139
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:46,470
By the end of twenty twenty-five, Singapore measured
seven hundred and forty-four square kilometers.

140
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:49,890
Twenty-eight percent bigger than in
nineteen sixty-five.

141
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:54,100
Since independence, it has added a
whole Washington, D.C.

142
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:55,250
of new land.

143
00:08:57,742 --> 00:09:04,232
But more land doesn't fix the problem Lee Kuan Yew
named on the day he broke down. Water.

144
00:09:04,837 --> 00:09:07,227
Under an agreement from
nineteen sixty-two,

145
00:09:07,957 --> 00:09:12,797
Singapore can take about three hundred
million US gallons of water a day from

146
00:09:12,837 --> 00:09:14,387
Malaysia's Johor River.

147
00:09:14,737 --> 00:09:19,167
The price: less than one US cent for
every thousand gallons.

148
00:09:19,790 --> 00:09:22,500
That agreement runs out in
twenty sixty-one.

149
00:09:22,990 --> 00:09:28,200
So for decades, Singapore has been building
what it calls the Four National Taps.

150
00:09:28,793 --> 00:09:30,483
Tap one: rain.

151
00:09:31,573 --> 00:09:35,583
Rivers and drains all over the island
feed into seventeen reservoirs.

152
00:09:36,233 --> 00:09:40,583
In twenty-oh-eight, Singapore even
dammed the mouth of Marina Bay,

153
00:09:40,893 --> 00:09:44,463
and the sea in front of the skyline
became a freshwater lake.

154
00:09:44,964 --> 00:09:48,194
Tap two: imported water from Johor.

155
00:09:48,764 --> 00:09:53,994
Tap three: NEWater, sewage cleaned so
thoroughly that it's ultra-pure.

156
00:09:54,564 --> 00:09:58,854
Most of it goes to factories, but some is
added back into the reservoirs.

157
00:09:59,626 --> 00:10:04,986
On National Day in two thousand two, about
sixty thousand people at the parade

158
00:10:05,026 --> 00:10:10,136
raised bottles of recycled sewage water and
drank a toast to the nation.

159
00:10:10,737 --> 00:10:13,327
And tap four: the sea itself.

160
00:10:14,517 --> 00:10:17,267
Singapore now has five
desalination plants.

161
00:10:17,917 --> 00:10:23,227
Today the country uses about five hundred and
thirty million US gallons of water a day.

162
00:10:23,737 --> 00:10:26,687
By twenty sixty-five, that
could almost double.

163
00:10:29,263 --> 00:10:30,893
And now there's a new problem.

164
00:10:31,503 --> 00:10:34,073
After sixty years of pushing the sea back,

165
00:10:34,403 --> 00:10:35,393
the sea is rising.

166
00:10:36,041 --> 00:10:41,431
Scientists in Singapore project that sea levels
could rise by up to one point one

167
00:10:41,471 --> 00:10:44,871
five meters, almost four feet, by
twenty-one hundred.

168
00:10:45,661 --> 00:10:49,871
With high tides and storms, the water
could reach four to five meters,

169
00:10:50,081 --> 00:10:52,831
up to sixteen feet, above today's average.

170
00:10:53,427 --> 00:10:57,977
About thirty percent of Singapore is less
than five meters above sea level.

171
00:10:58,487 --> 00:11:03,547
In twenty nineteen, Prime Minister Lee Hsien
Loong said protecting the coast could

172
00:11:03,587 --> 00:11:08,597
cost a hundred billion Singapore dollars over
the next fifty to a hundred years.

173
00:11:09,223 --> 00:11:12,333
So the next project is the
biggest idea yet.

174
00:11:12,943 --> 00:11:18,713
Off the East Coast, Singapore plans to build a
chain of new land called Long Island.

175
00:11:19,143 --> 00:11:23,293
About eight hundred hectares, around
twice the size of Marina Bay.

176
00:11:23,841 --> 00:11:27,131
It will be built high, as a
wall against the sea.

177
00:11:27,441 --> 00:11:31,271
And the water trapped behind it will
become a brand-new reservoir.

178
00:11:31,981 --> 00:11:35,871
Land, flood defense and drinking
water, in one project.

179
00:11:36,621 --> 00:11:39,851
Preparation work is planned to start
in twenty twenty-seven.

180
00:11:40,485 --> 00:11:45,665
In the far west, at Tuas, Singapore is also
building what will be one of the largest

181
00:11:45,705 --> 00:11:49,795
fully automated ports in the
world, on reclaimed land,

182
00:11:50,085 --> 00:11:53,275
with driverless vehicles and
remote-controlled cranes.

183
00:11:53,847 --> 00:11:56,237
So that's how Singapore became bigger.

184
00:11:56,747 --> 00:12:01,727
It moved its hills into the sea, bought half a
billion tonnes of other countries'

185
00:12:01,767 --> 00:12:05,317
sand, joined islands, dug
under the seabed,

186
00:12:05,487 --> 00:12:10,477
borrowed a trick from the Dutch, and turned its
sewage into water clean enough to drink.

187
00:12:11,025 --> 00:12:13,815
Would you live on land that
sits below the sea,

188
00:12:14,165 --> 00:12:15,595
kept dry by pumps?

189
00:12:16,105 --> 00:12:17,155
Tell me in the comments.

190
00:12:17,797 --> 00:12:22,167
It goes back to what one man said on a
Monday in nineteen sixty-five,

191
00:12:22,637 --> 00:12:24,227
minutes before he broke down.

192
00:12:24,637 --> 00:12:30,227
A country with no spare land and no water of its
own can't leave anything to chance.

193
00:12:30,537 --> 00:12:32,507
Not even the shape of its coastline.

194
00:12:33,151 --> 00:12:38,111
If you liked this, watch our story on how seven
and a half million people fit into

195
00:12:38,151 --> 00:12:39,241
Hong Kong next.

196
00:12:39,451 --> 00:12:43,441
And subscribe, so you don't miss how the
next place on Earth is run.
